# `val t = """x "q" y"""` - single quotes living inside a triple-quoted body.
#
# The gentler half of the greedy pair: a reader that closes on any `"` stops
# at byte 13 and reads `q` as an identifier. Extent [8, 21) off the text.
#
# `lacks simple_identifier` used to stand here and NO tree could ever satisfy
# it: the file's own `val t` binds a name, and a name is a `simple_identifier`.
# The claim meant "`q` is not read as an identifier" and said "this file
# contains no identifiers", which is false of the correct answer as well as the
# wrong one - a claim that cannot pass is not a test, it is a permanent red.
#
# Fixed by DELETING it rather than editing the file, and it cost this specimen
# nothing: `spans string_literal 8 21` was already standing on the next line
# and already says the whole thing. A reader that closes on the `"` at byte 13
# cannot also reach byte 21, so the span is false of the greedy reading and
# true of the right one - which is what the deleted line was reaching for and
# could not express. Checked both ways before removing it: against the tree
# before kotlin's strings were seated this specimen scores 0/5 with the span
# among the failures, and 5/5 after. Deleting an unsatisfiable claim is not
# the same act as weakening a live one, and the difference is exactly that
# demonstration.
roots 1
mends 0
holds string_literal
spans string_literal 8 21
